 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.06666666666666667,
   "dur": 4.568,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Why do you scar now? Before birth, a cut healed without one."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.433333333333334,
   "dur": 9.868,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Under the surface, most of your skin's strength is collagen, woven over and under in every direction like a basket, so it gives a little whichever way it's pulled."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 16.166666666666668,
   "dur": 11.017,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Cut through it, and the body doesn't reweave. It patches, laying new threads side by side, all in one direction, and leaves out the hair and the sweat glands."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 28.3,
   "dur": 9.457,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "We call a closed wound healed, as good as new. But pulled until it tears, even an old scar holds only about four-fifths of the original strength."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 40.43333333333333,
   "dur": 8.932,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Yet until about the sixth month of pregnancy, a cut in a baby's skin regrows the weave, follicles and all. After that, it patches."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 53.03333333333333,
   "dur": 6.336,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "And on most of your body, only the top layer, about this paper's thickness, grows back without a seam."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 60.5,
   "dur": 5.065,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "So, why the scar? Skin's quick repair: strong enough to close, never the weave."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 66.56666666666666,
   "dur": 3.765,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: a deadly poison, in a frown. How much goes in?"
  }
 ]
